1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:05,000
In the last lesson, you built an agent from scratch.

2
00:00:05,000 --> 00:00:09,000
Now, let's implement the agent using the link graph,

3
00:00:09,000 --> 00:00:13,000
and along the way, introduce some of its components and features.

4
00:00:13,000 --> 00:00:16,000
All right, let's get coding.

5
00:00:16,000 --> 00:00:21,000
All right, so let's break down what exactly we did in the last lesson.

6
00:00:21,000 --> 00:00:23,000
We had this user message that came in.

7
00:00:23,000 --> 00:00:27,000
We then had this system prompt, and this was this very long one.

8
00:00:27,000 --> 00:00:30,000
We then called an LLM with that.

9
00:00:30,000 --> 00:00:35,000
This outputted something like what's right there, a thought and an action.

10
00:00:35,000 --> 00:00:37,000
Based on that, we then made a decision.

11
00:00:37,000 --> 00:00:41,000
We would either return or we would call a tool.

12
00:00:41,000 --> 00:00:45,000
And we put this all in a big loop, this query function.

13
00:00:45,000 --> 00:00:50,000
And we had these two tools that we would call, calculate an average dogway.

14
00:00:50,000 --> 00:00:53,000
And if we called a tool, we would get that in observation

15
00:00:53,000 --> 00:00:56,000
and then would loop back in to the prompt,

16
00:00:56,000 --> 00:01:00,000
and would put that observation as a new message.

17
00:01:00,000 --> 00:01:03,000
So let's break this down into length chain components.

18
00:01:03,000 --> 00:01:05,000
First, let's talk about prompts.

19
00:01:05,000 --> 00:01:09,000
So prompt templates allow reusable prompts.

20
00:01:09,000 --> 00:01:15,000
And what this basically means is that we can create something like this right here.

21
00:01:15,000 --> 00:01:19,000
A little string with some formatted variables that we can replace.

22
00:01:19,000 --> 00:01:22,000
And so those formatted variables can come from user content.

23
00:01:22,000 --> 00:01:26,000
So we can have this prompt template that we format in different ways,

24
00:01:26,000 --> 00:01:28,000
depending on the user content.

25
00:01:28,000 --> 00:01:31,000
If you want to see a bunch of examples of these prompt templates,

26
00:01:31,000 --> 00:01:34,000
you can actually see them in the length chain hub.

27
00:01:34,000 --> 00:01:39,000
So here is one that's very similar to the agent prompt that we just used.

28
00:01:39,000 --> 00:01:42,000
So you can see here, it says, answer the following questions as best you can.

29
00:01:42,000 --> 00:01:44,000
You have access to the following tools.

30
00:01:44,000 --> 00:01:49,000
And here we don't hard code the tools themselves, but we have this little variable.

31
00:01:49,000 --> 00:01:52,000
And same with tool names down here.

32
00:01:52,000 --> 00:01:54,000
And then we have the input here.

33
00:01:54,000 --> 00:01:55,000
And this is the user question.

34
00:01:55,000 --> 00:01:56,000
And then the agent scratch pad.

35
00:01:56,000 --> 00:02:00,000
And these are all the actions it takes and the observations.

36
00:02:00,000 --> 00:02:03,000
If you want to see more prompts, you can go to the hub here.

37
00:02:03,000 --> 00:02:07,000
And you can see a bunch of prompts that people in the community have contributed.

38
00:02:07,000 --> 00:02:10,000
The next component in length chain that we have is tools.

39
00:02:10,000 --> 00:02:12,000
So here's a Taville tool.

40
00:02:12,000 --> 00:02:15,000
And this is the search that we will be using from here on out.

41
00:02:15,000 --> 00:02:18,000
You can see that we import it from the length chain community package,

42
00:02:18,000 --> 00:02:21,000
which contains hundreds of other tools.

43
00:02:21,000 --> 00:02:26,000
Maybe the biggest part of this application, the most code that we wrote,

44
00:02:26,000 --> 00:02:28,000
was this function that would loop.

45
00:02:28,000 --> 00:02:31,000
And that's represented by all these arrows here.

46
00:02:31,000 --> 00:02:34,000
And that's one way to think about Langgraph.

47
00:02:34,000 --> 00:02:38,000
Langgraph helps you describe and orchestrate that control flow.

48
00:02:38,000 --> 00:02:44,000
Specifically, it allows you to create cyclic graphs, which is exactly what we have here.

49
00:02:44,000 --> 00:02:47,000
It also comes with built in persistence.

50
00:02:47,000 --> 00:02:51,000
And this is really nice for having multiple conversations at the same time

51
00:02:51,000 --> 00:02:54,000
or remembering previous iterations and actions.

52
00:02:54,000 --> 00:02:59,000
This persistence also enables really cool human and the loop features.

53
00:02:59,000 --> 00:03:05,000
If you look at these diagrams here, and these are all diagrams of agents from academic papers,

54
00:03:05,000 --> 00:03:08,000
these are all represented as graphs.

55
00:03:08,000 --> 00:03:12,000
And that realization was what led us to create Langgraph,

56
00:03:12,000 --> 00:03:18,000
an extension of Langchain, specifically aimed at agent and multi-agent flows.

57
00:03:18,000 --> 00:03:22,000
Crucially, it allows for really controlled flows.

58
00:03:22,000 --> 00:03:29,000
So in these diagrams, there's really specific arrows leading from one box or state to the next.

59
00:03:29,000 --> 00:03:35,000
We've seen that this control ability is crucial for creating agents that can perform well.

60
00:03:35,000 --> 00:03:41,000
Three of the core concepts of Langgraph are nodes, edges, and conditional edges.

61
00:03:42,000 --> 00:03:45,000
Nodes are agents or functions.

62
00:03:45,000 --> 00:03:51,000
Edges connect these nodes, and then conditional edges are used when you need to make decisions

63
00:03:51,000 --> 00:03:55,000
about which node you should go to next.

64
00:03:55,000 --> 00:04:01,000
So let's take a look at an example of a Langgraph you can create that is equivalent

65
00:04:01,000 --> 00:04:04,000
to the function that we wrote in the lesson before.

66
00:04:04,000 --> 00:04:07,000
We can have an agent node. This is the LLM.

67
00:04:07,000 --> 00:04:13,000
We can then have a conditional edge, which takes the result of that LLM call and decides what to do next.

68
00:04:13,000 --> 00:04:18,000
One of those edges can be an action edge, which calls a function node,

69
00:04:18,000 --> 00:04:21,000
and that automatically loops back to the agent node.

70
00:04:21,000 --> 00:04:26,000
There's an entry point, which is where you start, and then there's the end node,

71
00:04:26,000 --> 00:04:30,000
which is the other action available to take after the agent.

72
00:04:30,000 --> 00:04:36,000
One of the most important things to understand when working with Langgraph is the state that is tracked over time.

73
00:04:36,000 --> 00:04:38,000
This is often called the agent state.

74
00:04:38,000 --> 00:04:44,000
This is accessible at all parts of the graph, at each node in each edge.

75
00:04:44,000 --> 00:04:48,000
It is local to the graph, and it can be stored into persistence layer,

76
00:04:48,000 --> 00:04:53,000
meaning that you can resume with that state at any point in time later on.

77
00:04:53,000 --> 00:05:01,000
Looking at two examples, we first have a simple state where we just have this list of messages.

78
00:05:01,000 --> 00:05:07,000
Taking a closer look, we can see that this message variable is a sequence of base message.

79
00:05:07,000 --> 00:05:13,000
Base message is a chain type. It's annotated with operator dot add.

80
00:05:13,000 --> 00:05:21,000
This means that when the state is updated with new messages, it doesn't override the existing messages,

81
00:05:21,000 --> 00:05:24,000
but rather it adds them to that state.

82
00:05:24,000 --> 00:05:27,000
Let's take a look at a more complex state.

83
00:05:27,000 --> 00:05:32,000
Here we have input, chat history, agent outcome, and intermediate steps.

84
00:05:32,000 --> 00:05:34,000
They all have their own different type.

85
00:05:34,000 --> 00:05:37,000
Three of them are not annotated in any way.

86
00:05:37,000 --> 00:05:44,000
This means that when a new update is pushed to that variable, it overrides the existing value there.

87
00:05:44,000 --> 00:05:48,000
However, intermediate steps is annotated.

88
00:05:48,000 --> 00:05:56,000
It's annotated with operator dot add, which means that when a new update is pushed there, it adds to it.

89
00:05:56,000 --> 00:06:04,000
This makes sense because intermediate steps is what is tracked as the agent action and agent observation throughout the graph as it executes.

90
00:06:04,000 --> 00:06:08,000
And so we want to continuously add to that as more and more actions are taken.

91
00:06:08,000 --> 00:06:15,000
Getting really specific about the previous agent that we created and how that would map to a lane graph object.

92
00:06:15,000 --> 00:06:18,000
We can see that we will have one node.

93
00:06:18,000 --> 00:06:22,000
We'll call this call open AI and this will call the lm.

94
00:06:22,000 --> 00:06:27,000
We'll then have a conditional edge, which will check for the existence of an action to take.

95
00:06:27,000 --> 00:06:29,000
And so we'll call this exists action.

96
00:06:29,000 --> 00:06:32,000
And then we'll have another node which actually executes that action.

97
00:06:32,000 --> 00:06:34,000
We'll call that take action.

98
00:06:34,000 --> 00:06:36,000
And the state that will track is pretty simple.

99
00:06:36,000 --> 00:06:40,000
It's just this list of messages to which we add over time.

100
00:06:40,000 --> 00:06:44,000
With that high level overview, let's dive into the code.

101
00:06:44,000 --> 00:06:48,000
Let's first load in any environment variables that we're going to need to use.

102
00:06:48,000 --> 00:06:51,000
This is our open AI API key.

103
00:06:51,000 --> 00:07:00,000
We're now going to import a bunch of things that we need to create these tools, agent state, and the lane graph itself.

104
00:07:00,000 --> 00:07:04,000
First, let's import state graph and this end node from lane graph.

105
00:07:04,000 --> 00:07:06,000
We'll see how we'll use those later on.

106
00:07:06,000 --> 00:07:10,000
These typing imports and operator are used to construct the agent state.

107
00:07:10,000 --> 00:07:13,000
We then have all these different message types.

108
00:07:13,000 --> 00:07:20,000
These are lane chain message types that we'll use to represent the human and AI and system messages.

109
00:07:20,000 --> 00:07:24,000
From lane chain open AI, we're going to import chat open AI.

110
00:07:24,000 --> 00:07:28,000
This is a lane chain wrapper around the open AI API.

111
00:07:28,000 --> 00:07:35,000
This exposes a standard interface for all language models, meaning that even though we're going to use chat open AI for this lesson,

112
00:07:35,000 --> 00:07:40,000
we can actually switch it out for any of the language model providers that lane chain supports,

113
00:07:40,000 --> 00:07:43,000
without having to change any other lines of code.

114
00:07:43,000 --> 00:07:51,000
Finally, we're going to import Taville, the search engine that we're going to start using as the tool in this example.

115
00:07:51,000 --> 00:07:53,000
Let's first take a look at the tool.

116
00:07:53,000 --> 00:07:59,000
We'll create the tool by initializing Taville search results with max results equals two.

117
00:07:59,000 --> 00:08:04,000
This means that we'll only get back two responses from the search API.

118
00:08:04,000 --> 00:08:11,000
We can see that it's of this type and it's got a specific name Taville underscore search underscore results underscore JSON.

119
00:08:11,000 --> 00:08:16,000
This is the name that the language model will use to call this tool.

120
00:08:16,000 --> 00:08:18,000
Let's now create the agent state.

121
00:08:18,000 --> 00:08:21,000
This is exactly what was in the presentation.

122
00:08:21,000 --> 00:08:26,000
It's just an annotated list of messages that we will add to over time.

123
00:08:26,000 --> 00:08:29,000
Let's now create our agent.

124
00:08:29,000 --> 00:08:32,000
As discussed, we're going to need three functions.

125
00:08:32,000 --> 00:08:41,000
We're going to need one function to call open AI, one function to check whether there's an action present and another function to take that action.

126
00:08:41,000 --> 00:08:44,000
These will all be different methods on this agent class.

127
00:08:44,000 --> 00:08:51,000
So let's create those first and we can then see how they interact with each other.

128
00:08:51,000 --> 00:08:53,000
Let's now create the agent class.

129
00:08:53,000 --> 00:08:57,000
We're going to want this agent to be parameterized by three different things,

130
00:08:57,000 --> 00:09:01,000
a model to use, the tools to call, and the system message.

131
00:09:01,000 --> 00:09:07,000
As before, let's save the system message as an attribute.

132
00:09:07,000 --> 00:09:10,000
We're now going to start creating the graph.

133
00:09:10,000 --> 00:09:17,000
First, let's initialize state graph with the agent state.

134
00:09:17,000 --> 00:09:19,000
Right now this graph is pretty bear.

135
00:09:19,000 --> 00:09:22,000
It doesn't have any nodes or edges attached to it.

136
00:09:22,000 --> 00:09:29,000
We know that we're going to want to create those three functions before and use them, two as nodes and one as edges.

137
00:09:29,000 --> 00:09:34,000
So let's sketch it out and then we'll implement those functions as methods on the class.

138
00:09:34,000 --> 00:09:40,000
So first, we know that we're going to want to create a node called LLM, which will execute the LLM.

139
00:09:40,000 --> 00:09:48,000
And we can do that by doing graph, add node, LLM.

140
00:09:48,000 --> 00:09:54,000
And then we're going to need to come back here and we're going to need to pass in the function that we want to represent this node.

141
00:09:54,000 --> 00:10:00,000
We'll add another node, the action node. We'll do the same thing here.

142
00:10:00,000 --> 00:10:02,000
Now we're going to add that conditional edge.

143
00:10:02,000 --> 00:10:05,000
This goes after the LLM is called.

144
00:10:05,000 --> 00:10:07,000
It checks whether there's an action present.

145
00:10:07,000 --> 00:10:10,000
If there is, then it goes to the action node.

146
00:10:10,000 --> 00:10:13,000
If there isn't, then it goes to the end node and it finishes.

147
00:10:13,000 --> 00:10:20,000
So let's add this. Again, we'll have to add the specific functions later on.

148
00:10:20,000 --> 00:10:27,000
The first argument we pass in is the node where the edge starts. This is from LLM.

149
00:10:27,000 --> 00:10:33,000
The second argument we pass in is the function that will determine where to go after that.

150
00:10:33,000 --> 00:10:41,000
We're going to implement this later on, so we'll leave this blank.

151
00:10:41,000 --> 00:10:53,000
The third and final argument that we're going to pass in is the dictionary representing how to map the response of the function to the next node to go to.

152
00:10:53,000 --> 00:10:57,000
So if the function returns true, then we're going to go to the action node.

153
00:10:57,000 --> 00:11:06,000
If it returns false, then we're going to go to the end node.

154
00:11:06,000 --> 00:11:12,000
We're now going to add a regular edge. This is going to go from the action node to the LLM node.

155
00:11:12,000 --> 00:11:24,000
So we can do this by calling add edge. The first argument is the start of the edge. The second argument is the end of the edge.

156
00:11:24,000 --> 00:11:32,000
We're then going to set an entry point for the graph. This is at the LLM node.

157
00:11:32,000 --> 00:11:42,000
With that done, we can compile the graph. Graph.compile is just what we need to call after we've done all this setups and we'll turn it into a lane chain runable.

158
00:11:42,000 --> 00:11:49,000
A lane chain runable exposes a standard interface for calling and invoking this graph. We'll see more about that later on.

159
00:11:49,000 --> 00:11:53,000
We'll save this as an attribute on the class.

160
00:11:53,000 --> 00:11:58,000
We'll also save the tools and the model that we passed in.

161
00:11:59,000 --> 00:12:07,000
For the tools, we'll create a dictionary, mapping the name of the tool to the tool itself.

162
00:12:07,000 --> 00:12:15,000
For the model, we'll actually call bind tools on the model, passing in the list of tools that we passed into the agent.

163
00:12:15,000 --> 00:12:21,000
What this is doing is this is letting the model know that it has these tools available to call.

164
00:12:22,000 --> 00:12:30,000
We've created this graph, but we still need to create three things. We need to create the function representing the LLM node.

165
00:12:30,000 --> 00:12:38,000
We need to create another function representing the action node, and we need to create a third function representing this conditional edge.

166
00:12:38,000 --> 00:12:43,000
We'll add all of these as methods on this agent class.

167
00:12:43,000 --> 00:12:48,000
For the LLM node, we're just going to create a function called call openAI.

168
00:12:48,000 --> 00:12:55,000
It's going to take in this state, this agent state, all of the nodes, and the edges will take this in.

169
00:12:55,000 --> 00:13:03,000
What we'll do is we'll get the list of messages from the state, we'll then add in this system message, and we'll then call the model.

170
00:13:03,000 --> 00:13:10,000
We'll then return this dictionary with a list of messages, but there's only one message in it. It's the message that's returned from the model.

171
00:13:10,000 --> 00:13:18,000
Again, remember because we had annotated the messages attribute on the agent state with the operator dot add.

172
00:13:18,000 --> 00:13:22,000
This isn't overwriting this, it's adding to that state.

173
00:13:22,000 --> 00:13:27,000
We can then go back to the graph dot add node and change that to pass in this method.

174
00:13:27,000 --> 00:13:31,000
Let's now do the same for the action node.

175
00:13:31,000 --> 00:13:34,000
The action node will also take in this agent state.

176
00:13:34,000 --> 00:13:38,000
We'll get the last message from the list of messages.

177
00:13:38,000 --> 00:13:44,000
We know that if we've got into this state, the language model must have wanted to call some tools.

178
00:13:44,000 --> 00:13:51,000
That means that there will be this tool calls attribute present on the last message in the agent state.

179
00:13:51,000 --> 00:13:54,000
Crucially, this can actually be a list of tool calls.

180
00:13:54,000 --> 00:13:59,000
So a lot of the more modern models support parallel tool or parallel function calling.

181
00:13:59,000 --> 00:14:03,000
What we can now do is we can loop over these tool calls.

182
00:14:03,000 --> 00:14:10,000
We can find the relevant tool by looking up tool name in the dictionary of tools that we created.

183
00:14:10,000 --> 00:14:17,000
We can then call invoke on it passing in the arguments that we have from this tool call.

184
00:14:17,000 --> 00:14:21,000
We can then append this as a tool message to this results list.

185
00:14:21,000 --> 00:14:26,000
And then we're returning this messages mapping to this results thing.

186
00:14:26,000 --> 00:14:30,000
Again, this is just the new messages that we need to add.

187
00:14:31,000 --> 00:14:37,000
What's happening under the hood is that length graph is adding that to the state in between iterations.

188
00:14:37,000 --> 00:14:46,000
Let's go back up and let's put this here in the action node.

189
00:14:46,000 --> 00:14:49,000
Finally, we need to define our conditional edge.

190
00:14:49,000 --> 00:14:55,000
So this is what's going to take in the result after the LLM is called and return a Boolean,

191
00:14:55,000 --> 00:15:00,000
true or false, representing whether we should take an action or whether we shouldn't.

192
00:15:00,000 --> 00:15:04,000
In order to do that, we're just going to get the last message from the state,

193
00:15:04,000 --> 00:15:07,000
which is the most recent call from the language model.

194
00:15:07,000 --> 00:15:12,000
And we're going to return length of that message dot tool calls greater than zero.

195
00:15:12,000 --> 00:15:15,000
So basically if there's any tool calls, we're going to return true.

196
00:15:15,000 --> 00:15:18,000
If there's none, then we're going to return false.

197
00:15:18,000 --> 00:15:22,000
Let's set this as the conditional edge.

198
00:15:22,000 --> 00:15:27,000
And with that, we have our agent. So let's start to use it.

199
00:15:27,000 --> 00:15:29,000
We create a nice little system prompt here.

200
00:15:29,000 --> 00:15:31,000
We initialize chat open AI.

201
00:15:31,000 --> 00:15:33,000
So this is the language model we're going to use.

202
00:15:33,000 --> 00:15:37,000
And for our list of tools that we're passing in, we're just passing in our one,

203
00:15:37,000 --> 00:15:40,000
our Taville search tool.

204
00:15:40,000 --> 00:15:45,000
One of the cool things we can do is we can actually visualize this graph we just created.

205
00:15:45,000 --> 00:15:51,000
So if we get the graph and then called docket graph and then dot draw PNG,

206
00:15:51,000 --> 00:15:55,000
we actually get an amazing visualization of the graph itself.

207
00:15:55,000 --> 00:15:59,000
This is all done automatically.

208
00:15:59,000 --> 00:16:01,000
So let's now call this agent.

209
00:16:01,000 --> 00:16:06,000
So let's first call it with this, what is the weather NSF question?

210
00:16:06,000 --> 00:16:11,000
In order to do this, we're going to create this human message representing a user message.

211
00:16:11,000 --> 00:16:14,000
We're then going to put it in this list of messages.

212
00:16:14,000 --> 00:16:19,000
And note that we have to do this because the state that the agent expects to work with

213
00:16:19,000 --> 00:16:23,000
has this messages attribute, which is a list of messages.

214
00:16:23,000 --> 00:16:26,000
So we need to make it conform with that state.

215
00:16:26,000 --> 00:16:31,000
Once we have this input, we can call the agent.graph.invoke with this.

216
00:16:31,000 --> 00:16:34,000
And we can get back a result.

217
00:16:34,000 --> 00:16:37,000
So we added some print statements in the agent.

218
00:16:37,000 --> 00:16:40,000
And so we can see that it printed out what it was calling.

219
00:16:40,000 --> 00:16:43,000
And it was calling to really search results JSON.

220
00:16:43,000 --> 00:16:47,000
And it was calling it with current weather NSF in San Francisco.

221
00:16:47,000 --> 00:16:49,000
And then it prints out back to the model.

222
00:16:49,000 --> 00:16:52,000
This means we're going back to the model if we look at result.

223
00:16:52,000 --> 00:16:56,000
If we look at result, we can see that it's a list of messages.

224
00:16:56,000 --> 00:17:00,000
So result is the final state that the agent ended up in.

225
00:17:00,000 --> 00:17:04,000
If we just want to get the final message in this list of messages,

226
00:17:04,000 --> 00:17:10,000
we can go into the messages attribute.

227
00:17:10,000 --> 00:17:13,000
Get the last one and look at the content attribute.

228
00:17:13,000 --> 00:17:18,000
And we can see that the current weather in San Francisco is partly cloudy blah blah blah.

229
00:17:18,000 --> 00:17:21,000
Let's now try this with more complicated question.

230
00:17:21,000 --> 00:17:25,000
So what is the weather in SF and LA?

231
00:17:25,000 --> 00:17:31,000
We can see that it first calls to really search results JSON with the current weather in San Francisco.

232
00:17:31,000 --> 00:17:34,000
Then before even going back to the model,

233
00:17:34,000 --> 00:17:39,000
it calls to really search results JSON again with current weather in Los Angeles.

234
00:17:39,000 --> 00:17:42,000
This is an example of parallel function or tool calling.

235
00:17:42,000 --> 00:17:44,000
Finally, it goes back to the model.

236
00:17:44,000 --> 00:17:46,000
And after that, it has all the information it needs.

237
00:17:46,000 --> 00:17:48,000
So it responds.

238
00:17:48,000 --> 00:17:52,000
And if we look at the final message,

239
00:17:52,000 --> 00:17:58,000
we can see that we get back our response from both San Francisco and Los Angeles.

240
00:17:58,000 --> 00:18:01,000
Let's try an even more complicated question.

241
00:18:01,000 --> 00:18:04,000
Who won the Super Bowl in 2024?

242
00:18:04,000 --> 00:18:08,000
What is the GDP of that state?

243
00:18:08,000 --> 00:18:14,000
We can see first it calls to Ville with the query 2024 Super Bowl winner.

244
00:18:14,000 --> 00:18:16,000
It then goes back to the model.

245
00:18:16,000 --> 00:18:18,000
It then calls to Ville again.

246
00:18:18,000 --> 00:18:22,000
This time with the query GDP of Missouri 2023.

247
00:18:22,000 --> 00:18:24,000
Then goes back to the model.

248
00:18:24,000 --> 00:18:26,000
And finally, it's done.

249
00:18:26,000 --> 00:18:28,000
And if we look at the final response,

250
00:18:28,000 --> 00:18:32,000
we can see that we get the Kansas City Chiefs won the Super Bowl in 2024.

251
00:18:32,000 --> 00:18:35,000
The GDP of Missouri, where the Kansas City Chiefs are based,

252
00:18:35,000 --> 00:18:40,000
was 400 something billion in the third quarter of 2023.

253
00:18:40,000 --> 00:18:45,000
So the difference here is that in order to make the second query,

254
00:18:45,000 --> 00:18:48,000
it actually had to know the result of the first query.

255
00:18:48,000 --> 00:18:50,000
So it calls one tool.

256
00:18:50,000 --> 00:18:55,000
Then goes back to the model in order to enable to call the second tool.

257
00:18:55,000 --> 00:18:58,000
This is a little bit different from parallel function calling.

258
00:18:58,000 --> 00:19:00,000
It's not happening in parallel.

259
00:19:00,000 --> 00:19:02,000
It's happening sequentially.

260
00:19:02,000 --> 00:19:05,000
And the reason it's happening sequentially is it actually needs the result of the first query

261
00:19:05,000 --> 00:19:08,000
in order to make the second query at all.

262
00:19:08,000 --> 00:19:13,000
With this, we've seen how we can take that raw LLM and raw Python example

263
00:19:13,000 --> 00:19:17,000
with some fake tools and turn it into a real agent that can answer complex questions.

264
00:19:17,000 --> 00:19:20,000
Are there the hoodies using the Ville search API?

265
00:19:20,000 --> 00:19:24,000
In the next lesson, we'll learn a lot more about that.
